Assignment context:

1. Lean Waste and Remedies

i) Waiting & Handoffs
Case-specific example:
The Process View and Development View could not be started until the Logical View was completed, because both relied on its classes and structure. The member working on the Process View was also waiting for the Logical View diagram to be finished before they could correct their own work. Meanwhile, the Sequence Diagram member had to ask to be informed whenever the UML changed, since each change affected the Sequence Diagram.
Impact:
Work that depended on the Logical View could not progress in parallel, which left less room to review and refine the dependent diagrams. Each UML change also required extra messages to keep the Sequence Diagram consistent.
Remedy:
Complete the Class Diagram first as a baseline, then start the dependent views from it. Once it is agreed, changes should be announced in the group chat before the dependent diagrams are updated.

ii) Defects and Rework
Case-specific example:
During the review, the Sequence Diagram and Use Case Diagram did not match the Class Diagram. For example, viewReward() appeared in the Sequence Diagram but not in the Class Diagram, and method names differed between the diagrams, such as manageClass() and manageClasses().
Impact:
The inconsistencies made the design unclear and required the affected diagrams to be corrected, which added extra effort to the report.
Remedy:
Treat the Class Diagram as the single source of truth. Before finalising, check every method and actor in the Sequence and Use Case Diagrams against it, and update the written explanations at the same time.

iii) Defects and Rework
Case-specific example:
The rewards requirement in the assignment instructions was initially overlooked, as it was assumed to be unnecessary. The reward feature had even been removed from the Sequence Diagram and Use Case Diagram because it was missing from the Class Diagram. When the requirement was noticed, the group had to add viewReward() back and adjust the related diagrams.
Impact:
Diagrams that had already been completed had to be reopened, which increased the amount of rework.
Remedy:
Read the full instructions together at the start and list every required feature in a checklist, then tick each one off against the diagrams before submission.

iv) Overprocessing
Case-specific example:
The instructions asked for one diagram per view, yet the group discussed whether to add a Data Flow Diagram, noting that other groups had drawn more than one. The Process View also ended up with two diagrams, which increased the page count and required adjustments to image sizes and layout.
Impact:
The extra diagram and the discussion took attention away from checking the required diagrams. The additional length also required formatting changes to keep the report within the page limit.
Remedy:
Decide the number of diagrams per view from the instructions before drawing anything, and add an extra diagram only if it covers something the required one cannot.

